# Mesh #5fe7fb4b — `.github/` IS scanned.
#
# It used to be excluded here, with the exclusion carrying a note that the 87
# findings inside it (75 `github-actions-mutable-action-tag`, 12
# `run-shell-injection`) were tracked separately rather than dismissed. They
# were, and they are fixed: every Action is pinned to a commit SHA, and every
# `${{ }}` that reached a `run:` body now reaches it through the environment.
#
# The exclusion is gone rather than kept-and-empty on purpose. `.github/`
# workflows still execute on the GitHub showcase mirror (hold-gate,
# showcase-migrated-gate, ci, trivy, memory-bench …) with a real GITHUB_TOKEN in
# scope, so the perimeter is live — it just stopped being the primary one when
# canon moved to GitLab (CLAUDE-task-workflow.md §0z). An excluded live
# perimeter is the shape where the next mutable tag lands unnoticed.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A repo-level .semgrepignore REPLACES semgrep's built-in defaults — it does not
# add to them. Measured on this tree (`semgrep scan --x-ls`, target-file count):
#
#   with this file, before the block below   24609 targets, 21716 in node_modules
#   with this file removed entirely           2548 targets,     0 in node_modules
#
# So the moment any job scans a checkout that has dependencies installed, an
# ignore file written as "just the one extra path we want excluded" silently
# turns the whole vendor tree into scan surface — minutes of runtime and
# findings in code we do not own. `:include .gitignore` pulls our own ignore
# rules back in; the literals below cover what .gitignore does not.
:include .gitignore

node_modules/
.git/
dist/
build/
coverage/
vendor/
*.min.js
*.min.css
